From f35fdbd622144f24d5d9fec216c64812a2ce57e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 20:33:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] HDR10+: put each percentile in the place of its percentage,
 or send none

The HDR10+ vendor-specific InfoFrame has nine places for the distribution of
maxRGB, each of one percentage (1, 5, 10, 25, 50, 75, 90, 95 and 99), and
no percentages of its own. MakeVsif() took the entries of the metadata by
their places and never looked at their percentages: a distribution of other
percentages went out with its values in the places of others, one of fewer
than nine entries with zeros, and one of ten in application version 1 with
its 98th percentile in the place of the 99th. The layout is that of the GPL
code of Amlogic (hdr10_plus_hdmitx_vsif_parser(), common_drivers
207374d38afd), which does the same: the first nine entries in their places,
and in version 0 with ten the tenth in the ninth (its 99th percentile).

DistributionFrom() now gives the entry of each place by its percentage, for
the two forms that hdr10plus_tool (MIT, read as data) takes: nine entries
at those percentages, or ten with 98 before 99, whose 99th goes in the ninth
place in either version. Any other distribution makes no InfoFrame: the
picture counts as one without HDR10+ metadata (CGap: the last InfoFrame for
fewer than 12 pictures, then plain HDR10), and the log says once a stream
why ("the metadata of a picture of this video cannot go in its InfoFrame:
..."), from the reason MakeVsif() gives through both decoders.

The InfoFrame of a stream of the usual form (nine entries) is byte for byte
what it was: the FATE metadata of the first HDR10+ host test give the same
27 bytes, and the test sample of 1.0 RC3 is of that form (ffprobe). What
changes: version 1 with ten entries (the 99th in the ninth place, not the
98th), and the forms above that now send none. The new host test,
hdr10plus_vsif_test.cpp, has 30 checks; 21 of them fail with the
HDR10PlusVsif.h before. Not read: the HDR10+ specification, which is given
to adopters only.
